window.__DOCS_SEARCH__ = {"generated":"2026-09-25","pages":[{"href":"index.html","title":"User Guide overview","lead":"This guide walks through the client's 11 feature pages one by one: what each page does, how to fill in parameters, and how to recover if something breaks. First-time users should start with \"Install and first run\".","sections":[{"id":"","title":"User Guide overview","level":1,"text":"This guide walks through the client's 11 feature pages one by one: what each page does, how to fill in parameters, and how to recover if something breaks. First-time users should start with \"Install and first run\". User Guide This guide walks through the client's 11 feature pages one by one: what each page does, how to fill in parameters, and how to recover if something breaks. First-time users should start with \"Install and first run\". Five-minute path to get started Install → import a subscription → flip its enable switch → turn on the system proxy → confirm on the Dashboard that the node is connected. Just five steps."},{"id":"a-few-terms-first","title":"A few terms first","level":2,"text":"Later chapters use these words constantly — take a minute to get familiar: Term Meaning Core The program doing the real work (mihomo). It's installed with the app; the client starts, supervises and stops it — you never install or configure it separately. Subscription An http/https link that opens to a YAML config containing nodes, policy groups and rules. The client downloads it as the raw material for generating the runtime config. Node / proxy group A node is a single line; a proxy group is a set of nodes that can auto-select by latency, switch manually or fail over. Rule The basis for deciding which line traffic takes. If nothing matches down to the last rule, the subscription's fallback policy applies (usually MATCH). System proxy Modifies Windows Internet settings so programs that honor the system proxy (browsers, most desktop software) use it automatically. Requires the core to be running. TUN mode Creates a virtual adapter to take over all traffic at the network layer, covering apps that ignore the system proxy (some games, command-line tools). Requires admin rights. Runtime config The config.yaml the client assembles from \"subscription + your overrides + app-level settings\" — the file the core actually reads. You never edit it directly."},{"id":"guide-contents","title":"Guide contents","level":2,"text":""},{"id":"getting-started","title":"Getting started","level":3,"text":"Install and first run — system requirements, installation steps, the data directory, and what to configure the first time."},{"id":"feature-guides-matching-the-left-menu","title":"Feature guides (matching the left menu)","level":3,"text":"Dashboard — overview status; switch proxy groups and nodes. Profiles — add, update and reorder subscriptions, enable several at once, import node links; Merge overrides and extension scripts. Proxies — switch nodes, test latency, favorites, region filters, chained proxies, one-click hiding of timed-out nodes. App Routing — route specific apps through specific lines. Rules — view active rules, rule sets and hit statistics. Connections — see what's online and create quick rules for domains. Traffic — daily up/down usage. Logs — logs for troubleshooting, filtered by level, persisted to files. Diagnostics — 11 checks and the network rescue kit. Unlock Test — check 18 services to judge whether the current route can stream and use AI. Settings — ports, mode, TUN, DNS, UWP tool, GeoData updates, traffic tunnel, node cleanup, shortcuts, theme customization, app logs, backup and diagnostics export."},{"id":"advanced-and-help","title":"Advanced and help","level":3,"text":"Advanced tips — Merge syntax, extension script examples, quick rules, command palette, floating window and shortcuts. Troubleshooting — symptoms, causes and fix order for common failures."},{"id":"where-every-setting-lives","title":"Where every setting lives","level":2,"text":"Can't find a switch? Check this table first — almost every switch lives in one of Settings' six tabs: Tab What it covers Appearance Theme, language, pause rendering when unfocused, Proxies page columns, hide timed-out nodes, theme customization (colors / fonts / custom CSS). System System proxy (with bypass list and terminal proxy commands), launch at startup, silent start, start page, startup script, tray click action, three-state tray icons, minimize to tray on close, traffic floating window, floating window mouse pass-through, system notifications, core watchdog, check for updates on launch, clear connections on node switch, system proxy guard, global shortcuts. Network Ports and mode (mixed proxy port, control port, mode, log level), kernel behavior (auto-start core, allow LAN, IPv6, unified delay), web dashboard (with control secret), TUN mode, custom DNS (with fallback DNS and static Hosts), speed test and health (scheduled latency tests, node health monitoring, auto-switch on failure), network environment (automatic system-proxy and mode switching by Wi-Fi name). Tools UWP App Tool, GeoData databases, traffic tunnel, node cleanup. Maintenance Subscription auto-update interval, core updates, node test URL and timeout, app logs, backup and restore (including export diagnostics). About App logo and name, app version, core version, data directory (openable in one click), help and support (website and online user guide), developer tools, quit the app. Settings apply instantly Every setting saves immediately — no \"OK / Apply\" buttons. Core-related changes (ports, TUN, DNS) hot-reload or restart the core automatically, and switches in the UI reflect the actual state."}]},{"href":"install.html","title":"Install and first run","lead":"From downloading the installer to confirming nodes are connected, this page lays out the complete order. Complete the \"five steps of first run\" below and the client is ready to use.","sections":[{"id":"","title":"Install and first run","level":1,"text":"From downloading the installer to confirming nodes are connected, this page lays out the complete order. Complete the \"five steps of first run\" below and the client is ready to use. User Guide From downloading the installer to confirming nodes are connected, this page lays out the complete order. Complete the \"five steps of first run\" below and the client is ready to use. Once installed, the Dashboard is the first thing you see: confirm the core is running and profiles and nodes are ready, then follow the steps below."},{"id":"system-requirements","title":"System requirements","level":2,"text":"Item Requirement Operating system Windows 10 / 11, 64-bit. Privileges TUN mode requires admin rights; enabling the system proxy likewise requires admin rights. If you only use the system proxy day to day, you can start with normal privileges and relaunch as administrator when you run into permission issues. Core No need to install the mihomo core yourself. The installer already bundles the mihomo core and the wintun TUN driver; the client starts and supervises them."},{"id":"installation","title":"Installation","level":2,"text":"Open the download page, get the Windows installer (NSIS format) from Releases, double-click it and follow the prompts. The current version is 0.1.1. On first run, if the data directory doesn't exist, the client tries migrating old configs from historical directory names so users upgrading from older versions keep their subscriptions and settings. Just use the default directory Keep the default directory the installer suggests; don't change it to another location."},{"id":"first-run-guide","title":"First-run guide","level":2,"text":"On a fresh install, the first launch opens a four-step guide that walks you through the initial setup: Welcome — a one-line intro to the app: subscription management, rule-based routing and traffic insights all happen locally, with no account needed. Import a subscription — paste your subscription link right in the guide to import it, or skip and add one later under Profiles. Quick setup — three recommended toggles: launch at login, system notifications and the core watchdog; change them anytime in Settings. Get started — a few handy tips: Ctrl+K opens the command palette, closing the window minimizes to the tray, and Diagnostics checks your network in one click. Every step has a Back button; after you click Skip or finish the guide it won't pop up again. Users upgrading from older versions are unaffected and won't see it."},{"id":"the-five-steps-of-first-run","title":"The five steps of first run","level":2,"text":"Go to Profiles, click \"Add subscription\" top-right, paste your http/https link (name can be left blank — the client detects it), and click OK. Flip the enable switch on the new subscription card (the first subscription is enabled automatically). A blue \"In use\" badge appears once it's on — multiple subscriptions can be enabled at the same time. Open Settings → Network and confirm \"auto-start core\" is on (on by default). The core then starts by itself whenever needed — no manual action required. Open Settings → General → System integration and turn on the \"system proxy\". Return to the Dashboard, confirm the core status is Running and the current node shows a latency number."},{"id":"three-ways-to-route-traffic-through-the-proxy","title":"Three ways to route traffic through the proxy","level":2,"text":"The client never forces a full takeover — choose the scope you need: Method Best for Notes System proxy The least hassle; covers browsers and most desktop programs. Modifies Windows Internet settings; requires the core to be running. TUN mode Full traffic takeover, covering apps that ignore the system proxy (some games, command-line tools). Requires admin rights. App Routing Only chosen apps use the proxy; everything else stays direct. Add per-app rules on the App Routing page."},{"id":"data-directory-and-backup","title":"Data directory and backup","level":2,"text":"All runtime data lives in %APPDATA%\\com.unboundlink.desktop, mainly: File / directory Contents settings.json All settings. profiles.json The subscription index. profiles\\{id}.yaml The original YAML of each subscription. merge.yaml Merge overrides. script.js Extension scripts. config.yaml The generated runtime config — the file the core actually reads. ui\\ Static files for the built-in web dashboard. rule_stats.json / traffic_history.json Rule hit statistics and traffic history. core_version.txt Core version records. Before migrating machines or reinstalling, open Settings → Maintenance → Backup and restore to export and import a JSON backup. It contains settings, subscriptions, Merge config and the core version. Two more things The app is single-instance — launching it again returns to the existing window. The main window, floating window and tray panel all hide rather than exit when closed; to really quit, use Settings → About → Quit."}]},{"href":"dashboard.html","title":"Dashboard","lead":"The Dashboard is the first screen you see after opening the client — check the overall status at a glance and switch proxy groups and nodes right there.","sections":[{"id":"","title":"Dashboard","level":1,"text":"The Dashboard is the first screen you see after opening the client — check the overall status at a glance and switch proxy groups and nodes right there. User Guide The is the first screen you see after opening the client — check the overall status at a glance and switch proxy groups and nodes right there. : the top row is the current subscription, current node and core status; the middle covers network setup, proxy mode and network checks; the bottom holds traffic stats and cumulative figures."},{"id":"what-this-page-solves","title":"What this page solves","level":2,"text":"Its role is the overview: is the core running, is traffic flowing normally, which node is active. The two most common everyday actions — check that you're connected and temporarily switch lines — both happen here, without visiting the Proxies page."},{"id":"core-status","title":"Core status","level":2,"text":"The core status card at the top shows whether the core is running, plus its version, memory usage and uptime — read by the client from the core so you can judge the background process's health. The sidebar footer also shows live \"Core memory\" and \"App memory\" usage, visible from anywhere. Running: the core is working; proxying is available. Not running: the core is down; the system proxy and nodes can't work."},{"id":"traffic-section","title":"Traffic section","level":2,"text":"The traffic section gives three summary figures and a live traffic chart: Item Meaning Total download Total traffic received since the core started this time. Total upload Total traffic sent since the core started this time. Active connections The number of connections in progress; jump to Connections for details. Live traffic chart Instant up/down speed trends, pushed from the core over WebSocket. Moving curves mean data is flowing Open a webpage or video and the curve should ripple while total download grows."},{"id":"switching-proxy-groups-and-nodes","title":"Switching proxy groups and nodes","level":2,"text":"The lower part of the Dashboard holds the proxy group / node switch rows. Each proxy group gets a row where you can pick its current node right here: Open a group's dropdown, pick a node, and the change takes effect immediately. For speed tests, favorites or finer filters, head to the Proxies page. A subscription usually has several proxy groups, like \"Select\" or \"Auto Select\". What you switch here is the group's current node; if the group uses an automatic policy it picks for itself, and a manual choice overrides the automatic result."},{"id":"common-uses","title":"Common uses","level":2,"text":"Task How to read / do it here Check that you're connected See whether total download / upload has values that grow while you browse, and confirm the current node shows a latency number. Temporarily switch lines Pick another node in the group's dropdown — no need to leave the page. Confirm the core is healthy Check the status card says Running and that uptime accumulates normally. When the core shows as not running First check Settings to confirm \"auto-start core\" is on; if it is but the core still isn't running, head to Diagnostics to find the problem."}]},{"href":"profiles.html","title":"Profiles","lead":"Manage your subscription sources here: add links, import local configs, enable one or more subscriptions, import single node links, update node lists, and fine-tune what you get from subscriptions with Merge and Script.","sections":[{"id":"","title":"Profiles","level":1,"text":"Manage your subscription sources here: add links, import local configs, enable one or more subscriptions, import single node links, update node lists, and fine-tune what you get from subscriptions with Merge and Script. User Guide Manage your subscription sources here: add links, import local configs, enable one or more subscriptions, import single node links, update node lists, and fine-tune what you get from subscriptions with Merge and Script. : each card shows usage progress, expiry date and days left, with update, usage and edit actions; manual nodes and the Merge / script entries sit at the bottom."},{"id":"page-overview","title":"Page overview","level":2,"text":"Below the title it shows \"N subscriptions · M enabled\" plus the auto-update status, e.g. \"Auto-update every 12 hours\" or \"Auto-update off\". Four buttons sit top-right: \"Update all\", \"Refresh\", \"Import local config\" and \"Add subscription\". Update all refreshes every subscription in turn; Refresh just re-reads local state; Import local config adds a local YAML file to the list as a subscription."},{"id":"adding-a-subscription","title":"Adding a subscription","level":2,"text":"Click \"Add subscription\" to open the dialog — just two fields: Field Notes Subscription URL Required; must be an http/https link. Submitting empty shows \"Please enter a subscription URL\". Name Optional; the client detects it automatically when left blank."},{"id":"what-each-card-can-do","title":"What each card can do","level":2,"text":"Each subscription is a card; press and hold to drag-reorder. Cards show the name, a blue \"In use\" badge on every enabled subscription (several can appear at once), the URL (truncated when long), a relative \"Updated …\" time, and subscription info (traffic usage, expiry, etc., when the provider supplies it). The right side holds the enable switch followed by the action buttons: Button Action Enable switch Flip it on to enable this subscription; several can be enabled at once — every enabled subscription carries the blue \"In use\" badge. Update Re-downloads this subscription's content. Traffic Refreshes subscription info to check usage and expiry. QR code Generates a QR code to import on your phone. History Lists the archived versions of this subscription so you can roll back to any of them (not available for local configs). Website Appears when the provider's website is detected; click to open it in your default browser. The dot beside the icon shows reachability (green = reachable, red = unreachable) — hover to see check details. Edit Edits the subscription name and the provider's website (if none was detected, fill it in here; leave empty to restore auto-detection). Move up / down Reorders the subscription in the list. Delete Red button; asks \"Delete this subscription? This cannot be undone.\""},{"id":"multiple-subscriptions-at-once","title":"Multiple subscriptions at once","level":2,"text":"Several subscriptions can be enabled at once: just flip the enable switch at the top-right of a card, and every enabled subscription carries the blue \"In use\" badge. The first enabled subscription supplies the base config — port, DNS, run mode — while the others merge their nodes, proxy groups and rules into the same runtime config; the effective order follows the list from top to bottom, so drag cards to reorder."},{"id":"version-history","title":"Version history","level":2,"text":"Before every remote update, the old content is archived automatically, keeping up to 5 versions. Click the clock icon on a subscription card to open the version history dialog, which shows each snapshot's time and node count; click Restore to roll back to that version. If the subscription is enabled, rolling back hot-reloads the running config immediately; the current content is archived first, so you can always undo the rollback. Local configs don't take part in remote updates, so they have no version history. After an update the card shows change badges for node and rule counts (green for growth, red for shrink), and a warning pops up if the node count drops by more than half."},{"id":"importing-a-local-config","title":"Importing a local config","level":2,"text":"Click \"Import local config\" and pick a local YAML file; the client copies it into the data directory and adds it to the list as a subscription, tagged \"Local\" and labeled \"local config file (excluded from remote updates)\". Handy for configs you maintain yourself or got from elsewhere — no need to upload to a server first. Local subscriptions take part in neither remote updates nor auto-update; \"Update all\" skips them. The file lives in the data directory — re-import it when moving to a new version. Success shows \"Local config imported\"."},{"id":"manual-nodes","title":"Manual Nodes","level":2,"text":"The \"Manual Nodes\" card at the bottom of the page holds single nodes outside any subscription: click \"Import links\", paste the node links into the dialog (one per line), click \"Parse\" to preview, then \"Import N\" to finish. Common protocols are supported — ss / vmess / vless / trojan / hysteria2 / tuic / socks5 / http; failed lines are reported individually and duplicate names are skipped automatically. Manual nodes live in the data directory and merge into a proxy group of the same name when the runtime config is generated (default group name: \"Manual Nodes\"). Remove imported nodes one by one on the card; changes save immediately and hot-reload the config."},{"id":"global-override-config-merge","title":"Global override config (Merge)","level":2,"text":"At the bottom sits the \"Global override config\" card with a Merge tag. Open it and write a YAML snippet — when the runtime config is generated it's deeply merged recursively with the subscription to override its fields (app-level settings such as port, control secret and mode are written by the client — writing them here has no effect; change them under Settings → Network). Saving shows \"Merge config saved and applied\"."},{"id":"global-extension-script-script","title":"Global extension script (Script)","level":2,"text":"Next to it, the \"Global extension script\" card with a Script tag. Open it and write JavaScript implementing function main(config){ … return config }. The client runs it on the built-in QuickJS engine to batch-rename nodes, add or remove rules and more. Saving shows \"Extension script saved and applied\". A sample script using regex to strip ads from node names comes built in."},{"id":"auto-update","title":"Auto-update","level":2,"text":"The auto-update interval is set under Settings → Maintenance → Subscription, in hours, 0 means off (off by default). Once enabled, every subscription in the list updates on schedule in the background; when done, the config reloads automatically and a system notification is sent. Two subscription-linked switches live in the same place: \"Auto-optimize after update\" is off by default — once enabled, every time a subscription update finishes and the config reloads, it tests the main proxy group and switches the current node to the lowest-latency one; \"Profile usage alerts\" is on by default and sends a system notification when used traffic reaches the threshold, remaining days run low or the subscription has expired, each tier firing only once. Both rely on the traffic and expiry info provided by the subscription source. If updates fail, check the network first Failed updates usually mean a dead URL or a broken connection. Check the network in Diagnostics, then come back and retry \"Update\"."}]},{"href":"proxies.html","title":"Proxies","lead":"This page browses the nodes and groups your subscriptions bring, switches lines, tests latency, favorites nodes and configures chained proxies for restricted nodes.","sections":[{"id":"","title":"Proxies","level":1,"text":"This page browses the nodes and groups your subscriptions bring, switches lines, tests latency, favorites nodes and configures chained proxies for restricted nodes. User Guide This page browses the nodes and groups your subscriptions bring, switches lines, tests latency, favorites nodes and configures chained proxies for restricted nodes. : groups and node providers on the left; node cards on the right show latency and protocol, with \"Entry / Exit\" badges marking chained nodes. The toolbar searches, filters, switches tabs and speed-tests everything in one click."},{"id":"page-layout","title":"Page layout","level":2,"text":"A column on the left lists entries: groups, node sources (providers / subscriptions) and other groups. Click an entry to show its nodes on the right. Each entry has a ⟳ icon that \"updates the node list from the source right now\", re-fetching the nodes under that entry. Group entries carry notes like: other groups pick routes by their own policy, e.g. auto-selecting the lowest-latency node. Their routes come from the subscription — no need to specify them one by one."},{"id":"search-and-sorting","title":"Search and sorting","level":2,"text":"The toolbar at the top-right narrows the range and changes display order. The search box covers groups, nodes and providers, and accepts region abbreviations like hk. The sort dropdown offers default order, sort by latency and sort by name. The difference: search filters — only matches remain; sorting just reorders without removing anything. Tools Action Search box Searches groups, nodes and providers; accepts region abbreviations like hk. Sort dropdown Default order / by latency / by name. Hide timed-out Nodes that timed out in tests are hidden for a cleaner list; the switch remembers its state and is the same setting as \"hide timed-out nodes\" in Settings. Refresh Re-reads the nodes under the current entry. Test all Tests latency for every node under the current entry."},{"id":"filter-bar","title":"Filter bar","level":2,"text":"Below the toolbar sits the filter bar with region pills (including \"All\") and \"Favorites only\". Region pills show nodes from one region; Favorites only shows starred nodes. Drag the bar sideways to pan through it — no need to squeeze many regions onto one screen."},{"id":"map-view","title":"Map view","level":2,"text":"A \"List / Map\" toggle sits at the top-right of the node list. Switch to map view and a world map appears on the right, with nodes aggregated into dots by country and region: the region a dot lands in is where your nodes are, so the global spread reads at a glance. Dot size reflects how many nodes a region has — bigger means more. Color reflects the best latency within the region: green is fast, orange is moderate, red is slow, and grey means no node there has been latency-tested yet. The region of the node currently in use is ringed with a highlight to locate the active line at a glance. Hover a dot to see the region name, node count and best latency. Click any dot to filter nodes by that region: the view switches back to the list, the matching region pill in the filter bar becomes selected, and only that region's nodes remain on the right — \"see the map, drill into the list\" in one step. Click \"All\" in the filter bar to restore everything. The list and the map share the same nodes and filters, so switching back and forth loses nothing. Map data is built into the app; rendering needs no network and no online map service."},{"id":"node-cards","title":"Node cards","level":2,"text":"Each node card shows the node name and latency (color-coded so speed reads at a glance). Clickable elements on the card: Element Action Star Favorites the node; favorites always float to the top. Lightning button Tests this node's latency. Gauge button Download speed test, about 10 seconds. Chain badge Nodes in a chain show role tags after their names: Entry (first hop) / Relay / Exit (final landing); hover to see the order. Latency trend With Node health monitoring on, each card carries a recent latency line (red dots mark failed tests), so you can tell at a glance whether a node is stable."},{"id":"the-two-kinds-of-speed-tests","title":"The two kinds of speed tests","level":2,"text":"The lightning button runs a latency test — the same figure shown on the card, good for quickly comparing who's closer. The gauge button runs a download speed test, taking about 10 seconds; during the test the exit switches to that node temporarily and restores the previous node afterwards, so there's nothing to switch back manually. To triage a large batch of nodes, \"Test all\" in the toolbar is easier."},{"id":"about-test-results","title":"About test results","level":2,"text":"Test results stay valid for about 10 minutes, then clear automatically so stale data doesn't mislead. A node with no latency number usually just has expired results — test again. To keep timed-out nodes out of the list, turn on \"Hide timed-out\" at the top of the page. With Node health monitoring enabled (Settings → Network), every test result is recorded on a rolling basis and drawn as a latency trend line on the node card: the line reflects recent latency levels and red dots mark failed tests; if the node in use keeps failing tests, the app sends a system notification and, with Automatic failover on, also switches the line to the fallback node (or the lowest-latency available node in the group) and reports the result. Right after you enable monitoring there's little data, so the line fills in as you test more or as scheduled tests run."},{"id":"chained-proxy","title":"Chained proxy","level":2,"text":"Some nodes are restricted or unstable when reached directly; give them a front relay so traffic goes through the relay first and exits here — one such relationship is a \"chain\". Click \"Chained proxy rules\" in the toolbar to open the chain editor (a drawer on the right): saved chains on the left, a visual builder on the right, with a complete chain shown as \"This device → Hop 1 → Hop 2 → Internet\". To build a chain: click \"New chain\" and click hops in the candidate list below to add them in order. The list supports search and region filters, pins favorites on top, shows each node's latest latency on the right (from the core's test records; untested nodes show none), and pools all direct nodes — no need to switch left-side groups when picking across them. Click a hop again to remove it; drag hop blocks to reorder; click the + between hops to set the insertion point, and the next hop you click goes there. Element Notes Proxy group Can only be the chain's first hop (entry), e.g. \"HK Auto → landing node\"; regular nodes can go anywhere. Drag Press and drag a hop block to reposition it in the chain. × button Removes the hop; the + between hops sets where the next hop is inserted. Invalid marker Hops no longer present in the enabled subscriptions are flagged in red; \"Remove invalid chains\" cleans them up in one click, applied on save. Clicking \"Save and apply\" writes the whole chain and hot-reloads the runtime config at once — effective immediately. To use it, select the chain's last node and the full path is used; selecting a middle hop passes only through the hops before it. The first hop alone isn't a chain (it's a relay referenced by later hops). Chained latency is roughly the sum of all hops — usually slower, but potentially more stable. To quickly give one node a front proxy, still click the chain button on its card and pick a front node or group in the dialog; such quick rules also appear in the chain editor as chains and remain editable. Switching nodes applies instantly Switching nodes on the Dashboard or this page hot-reloads the config — no core restart, no stop-and-start needed."}]},{"href":"apps.html","title":"App Routing","lead":"Route different apps through different lines: bind your browser, games and download tools to suitable lines, each independent of the others.","sections":[{"id":"","title":"App Routing","level":1,"text":"Route different apps through different lines: bind your browser, games and download tools to suitable lines, each independent of the others. User Guide Route different apps through different lines: bind your browser, games and download tools to suitable lines, each independent of the others. : each row binds an app to target routes — Chrome goes through \"Select Node\", Telegram through \"AI Route\", and OBS aggregates 2 routes. The page subtitle says it: route different apps through different lines. The \"Enable \" master switch sits at the top, off by default, with an \"Add app binding\" button above the table. The master switch gates this whole page — bindings you add below only take effect once it's on. Each binding connects one app to one or more lines, after which that app's traffic follows the bound lines."},{"id":"the-binding-table","title":"The binding table","level":2,"text":"All bindings are listed in a table, one binding rule per row. Column Meaning App Shows the icon and name / process name. Target lines Switch lines directly in the row. With multiple lines it shows an aggregation note — \"multiple connections are distributed across the lines; click Edit to change\" — plus the latest latency for that line. Live speed Up/down speed of the app's current active connections, from the core's connection records. Enabled Toggles whether this binding is active. Actions Edit and remove. Removal asks for confirmation: \"Remove this app binding?\". The Target lines column switches right in the row — no need to open the dialog every time; only aggregated multi-line targets require Edit to re-select. Live speed comes from the core's connection records and shows the app's currently active up/down traffic; it drops to zero when connections close, so it reflects this moment, not cumulative usage. To refresh line latency, use the \"refresh lines\" style button in the toolbar."},{"id":"adding-an-app-binding","title":"Adding an app binding","level":2,"text":"Click \"Add app binding\" to open the dialog; fill in three parts: app, match method and target lines. Field Notes App Enter a process name (e.g. chrome.exe) or full path (e.g. C:\\Games\\game.exe); a \"Choose program file\" button lets you browse for the exe; you can also pick directly from the list of running processes the core has seen making network connections, complete with app icons and window titles. Match method \"By process name (recommended)\" or \"By full path\". Target lines Multi-select; choosing 2 or more aggregates them automatically for more speed. Options include policy groups, nodes and two fixed entries: \"Direct (no proxy)\" and \"Reject (block connection)\". Aggregation policy Appears only with 2+ lines: \"round-robin\" (connections spread across lines automatically) or \"session-persistent\". There are three ways to fill in the app: type a process name, type a full path, or click \"Choose program file\" to browse for the exe. If unsure, pick straight from the running-process list (processes the core has seen currently or recently making connections) — icons and window titles make it easier to find the right one. Then the match method decides how the app is recognized: \"By process name (recommended)\" is shorter and more tolerant of moved or reinstalled programs; \"By full path\" is more precise."},{"id":"target-lines-and-aggregation","title":"Target lines and aggregation","level":2,"text":"Target lines is a multi-select. Choices include policy groups, nodes and two fixed entries: \"Direct (no proxy)\" and \"Reject (block connection)\". With 2+ lines the client creates an aggregation group so several lines serve the app at once; with one line, traffic takes just that line. Two or more lines also prompt for an aggregation policy: round-robin spreads connections across lines, while session-persistent keeps a session on one line."},{"id":"common-validation-messages","title":"Common validation messages","level":2,"text":"If saving fails, the dialog shows one of these messages — fix accordingly: Message Meaning Select or enter an app process No app selected. Select at least one target line No line selected. This app is already in the binding list Duplicate binding. Aggregation doesn't support Direct or Reject; they were removed automatically — to use Direct, select it alone The aggregation included Direct or Reject. Process name or line name contains a comma A warning is shown because commas are written into rules."},{"id":"typical-setups","title":"Typical setups","level":2,"text":"Browser through \"Auto Select\", games through a \"low-latency line\", download tools pinned to a single fixed line; or set a chat app to \"Reject\" to block its connectivity. A few rules of thumb: apps that prize stability get a single line; apps that prize speed get multiple lines aggregated; apps that shouldn't use the proxy get \"Direct\"; apps that shouldn't be online at all get \"Reject\". Routing only affects bound processes App Routing affects only bound processes; all other traffic still follows the rule list. Bindings depend on the core running — they don't take effect while the core is stopped."}]},{"href":"rules.html","title":"Rules","lead":"The Rules page answers \"which line does which traffic take\": it lists the rules in effect, the rule sets your subscription references, and how often each rule is actually hit.","sections":[{"id":"","title":"Rules","level":1,"text":"The Rules page answers \"which line does which traffic take\": it lists the rules in effect, the rule sets your subscription references, and how often each rule is actually hit. User Guide The page answers \"which line does which traffic take\": it lists the rules in effect, the rule sets your subscription references, and how often each rule is actually hit. : four tabs — , Custom rules, Rule sets and Hit statistics; the table lists type, match content, policy and hit count, with quick-rule chips and the route simulator up top."},{"id":"four-tabs","title":"Four tabs","level":2,"text":"Four tabs at the top: Rules, Custom rules, Rule sets and Hit statistics — different facets of the same rule set: \"what rules exist\", \"which extra rules I want\", \"where they come from\" and \"how well they're used\". Switch tabs to view each without leaving the page."},{"id":"rules","title":"Rules","level":2,"text":"The default first tab lists every rule in the current runtime config. Data comes from the exact config the core reads — your subscription and overrides combined. Four columns: Column Meaning Type The match type, e.g. DOMAIN-SUFFIX, GEOIP and so on. Match The domain, IP range or region this rule matches. Policy (exit) Where matched traffic exits — usually a proxy group, a node name, or a built-in policy like DIRECT or REJECT. Hits How many times this rule has been hit in total. Rule counts are often large, so filtering and search sit above: filter by type or search the match content by keyword. Long results paginate automatically to help you find a specific rule."},{"id":"custom-rules","title":"Custom rules","level":2,"text":"The Custom rules tab is a visual editor: add, edit and reorder your own routing rules without hand-writing config files. On Save & apply they are injected at the very top of the runtime rules in list order, outranking quick rules and subscription rules. Editing is draft-style: changes stay on the page, a Discard changes button and an unsaved-changes note appear, and nothing is written until you click Save & apply, which hot-reloads the core; Discard changes restores the saved state. The table shows all rules in the draft with six columns: #, Type, Match, Policy, Extra and Actions. Each row has four buttons: Move up, Move down, Edit and Delete; higher rows have higher priority, and Move up on the first row and Move down on the last are disabled. Click Add rule or a row's Edit button to open a form with four fields: Field Notes Rule type e.g. DOMAIN-SUFFIX, GEOSITE, IP-CIDR; type freely or pick from the common suggestions — values are upper-cased automatically. Match The domain, IP range or region to match. Not needed for the MATCH type. Target The exit on a hit: DIRECT, REJECT, or a proxy group / node name from the running core (pick from the suggestions). no-resolve Shown only for IP-type rules (IP-CIDR, GEOIP and the like); when on, these rules skip DNS resolution to avoid needless lookups. Rule types are limited to upper-case letters, digits and hyphens, and Match / Target must not contain commas or line breaks; if any rule fails validation the whole save is rejected with the offending row number. Up to 200 custom rules. A MATCH rule truncates everything after it Custom rules go to the very top of the rule list, and matching runs top to bottom — the first hit wins. Put a MATCH rule first and nothing below it will ever match."},{"id":"rule-sets","title":"Rule sets","level":2,"text":"The Rule sets tab lists the rule sets your subscription references — rule-provider entries. Rule sets bundle large batches of rules into external files to keep the main config lean; once referenced, their rules match alongside the rest. Five columns: Column Meaning Name The rule set's identifying name. Type The rule set's type. Behavior The rule set's match behavior. Rules How many rules the set contains. Updated When the rule set was last updated."},{"id":"hit-statistics","title":"Hit statistics","level":2,"text":"The Hit statistics tab presents rules by actual usage in four columns: Type, Match, Exit and Hits, so you can see which rules really pull their weight. The client samples connection records every 15 seconds while the core runs and accumulates them to disk, so right after launch it may show \"No statistics yet (sampled every 15 seconds while the core runs)\" — data trickles in shortly after. Statistics are persisted and survive restarts."},{"id":"route-simulator","title":"Route simulator","level":2,"text":"The toolbar on the Rules page has a Route simulator button: type a domain, IP or process name and it replays locally which rule would match and what its policy is. No running core is required — very handy for working out why a site went the way it did. Two input modes are supported: Domain / IP matches domain- and IP-based rules; App process matches process rules (e.g. chrome.exe). Results show the matched rule number, its text and policy; if the policy is a proxy group, the group's current exit node is resolved too. A few rules cannot be decided locally (those relying on GeoSite / GeoIP data, regex, or pending domain resolution); the simulator lists them as skipped instead of giving a wrong answer, and malformed input gets its own hint. Every decision is based on the current runtime config — the same rules the core actually uses."},{"id":"rule-priority","title":"Rule priority","level":2,"text":"Rules match top to bottom; the first hit wins. In the runtime config the order is: custom rules (arranged in the Rules page editor) → quick rules (right-click on the Connections page) → subscription rules. Both in-app sources sit above subscription rules, with custom rules highest. App routing bindings match by process and also apply at config generation time. Changed rules but nothing changed? First check the mode is \"Rule\": Settings → Network → Mode. In Global mode all traffic takes one exit and rules have no effect."}]},{"href":"connections.html","title":"Connections","lead":"The Connections page shows what's online right now and lets you create rules for domains on the fly — make a domain direct or route it through a chosen line immediately, without touching config files.","sections":[{"id":"","title":"Connections","level":1,"text":"The Connections page shows what's online right now and lets you create rules for domains on the fly — make a domain direct or route it through a chosen line immediately, without touching config files. User Guide The page shows what's online right now and lets you create rules for domains on the fly — make a domain direct or route it through a chosen line immediately, without touching config files. : each row shows host, process, network, destination, matched rule and final chain; turn on \"Group by host\" or filter by process, and create rules for a domain on the spot."},{"id":"what-this-page-solves","title":"What this page solves","level":2,"text":"When you're unsure who's online or which line a site took, come here. It lists every connection the core is handling right now, so you can verify routing results and fix them in place: right-click a connection to create a rule for its domain."},{"id":"two-views","title":"Two views","level":2,"text":"Switch between two ways of viewing the page: Detail view — one row per connection, ideal for inspecting a single connection. Columns: Column Meaning Host The connection's destination hostname. Process The program that opened the connection. Network The network protocol in use — tcp or udp. Destination The address actually visited. Rule The rule this connection matched. Chain Which line or proxy group the traffic took. Up / Down Data sent / received by this connection. Duration How long the connection has lasted. Aggregated view — merges similar connections by domain / destination to reveal the overall picture. Columns: domain / destination, connections, process, rule, chain, up, down."},{"id":"create-quick-rules-with-right-click","title":"Create quick rules with right-click","level":2,"text":"Right-click any connection row to open a menu with: Direct for this domain — traffic to this domain goes out directly, bypassing the proxy. Route this domain through a chosen line — a submenu lists available proxy groups; pick one and the domain routes through it. With no groups available the submenu shows \"No proxy groups available\". Copy domain — copies the connection's domain to the clipboard. Copy destination — copies the connection's destination address. Copy process path — copies the full path of the process that opened the connection."},{"id":"how-quick-rules-take-effect","title":"How quick rules take effect","level":2,"text":"Choosing \"Direct for this domain\" or \"Route this domain through a chosen line\" creates a quick rule of type DOMAIN-SUFFIX. It's inserted at the very top of the rules when the runtime config is generated, so it outranks subscription rules and takes effect for that domain immediately — no core restart needed. To undo, right-click the domain again and pick a different line. Two practical tips Live speeds come from the core and disappear once a connection closes. With lots of data, use the search / filter above to narrow things down and find your target faster."}]},{"href":"stats.html","title":"Traffic","lead":"The Traffic page records this machine's daily up and down traffic through the core, so you can review usage trends over time.","sections":[{"id":"","title":"Traffic","level":1,"text":"The Traffic page records this machine's daily up and down traffic through the core, so you can review usage trends over time. User Guide The page records this machine's daily up and down traffic through the core, so you can review usage trends over time. : totals for download / upload, overall traffic, daily average and the peak day up top; a download / upload bar chart in the middle; per-day and per-app tables below."},{"id":"what-this-page-solves","title":"What this page solves","level":2,"text":"It answers \"how much traffic did I use these days\". All data comes from the local core — no server collects it; what you see is this computer's actual usage."},{"id":"choosing-a-time-range","title":"Choosing a time range","level":2,"text":"The top of the page switches between four time ranges: Last 7 days — usage over the past week. Last 30 days — usage over the past month. Last 90 days — usage over the past three months. All — every record in history. After switching, the chart and table below update in sync. Shorter ranges show clearer trends; longer ranges are better for overall scale. \"Last 7 days\" is enough for daily checks — reach for \"Last 30 days\" or \"Last 90 days\" to see long-term usage. Switching only affects the display; no historical data is deleted."},{"id":"what-to-look-at-and-how","title":"What to look at and how","level":2,"text":"The page presents data two ways at once — a trend chart and a detail table: the chart shows the curve's ups and downs so you can spot high-traffic days at a glance; the table gives each day's exact numbers. The table has four columns: Column Meaning Date The day this row covers. Download Total traffic received that day. Upload Total traffic sent that day. Total Combined up and down traffic for the day. If the current range has no records yet, the page shows \"No records in this range\". Widen the range, or come back after using the app for a while."},{"id":"traffic-by-app","title":"Traffic by app","level":2,"text":"At the bottom of the page there is an App traffic panel that groups traffic by originating process, showing which app used the most bandwidth over the period. The table has five columns — app, download, upload, total and last active — sorted by total, highest first. Data is sampled from connection records every 15 seconds while the core runs; only connections with an identifiable process are counted. The list may be empty right after start-up and fills in shortly. Totals persist across restarts. To start over, click Reset at the panel's top-right to clear all per-app totals; daily records are not affected."},{"id":"traffic-by-node","title":"Traffic by node","level":2,"text":"Below the App traffic panel there is a Node traffic panel that groups traffic by the exit node each connection finally leaves through, with direct traffic listed separately as a DIRECT row — handy for comparing how much went through the proxy and how much went direct. The table has five columns — node, download, upload, total and last active — sorted by total, highest first. Data is likewise sampled every 15 seconds while the core runs and accumulated; totals persist across restarts. To start over, click Reset at the panel's top-right to clear all per-node totals; daily records and App traffic are not affected."},{"id":"where-the-data-is-stored","title":"Where the data is stored","level":2,"text":"Data is recorded continuously while the client runs and stored in the data directory: daily records in traffic_history.json, per-app traffic in app_traffic.json and per-node traffic in node_traffic.json. Therefore history is kept after a restart and is not lost when you exit. All these files are maintained automatically by the client — no manual editing needed. Only traffic through the core is counted Only traffic that goes through the core is counted. Direct connections that bypass the core aren't included, so the totals may be lower than the adapter's actual usage. About clearing records Daily records have no dedicated clear button — you can overwrite them indirectly by importing a backup under Settings → Maintenance → Backup & restore. Per-app and per-node totals can be cleared with Reset at each panel's top-right."}]},{"href":"logs.html","title":"Logs","lead":"Logs record all output from the client and the core — the most direct evidence when troubleshooting.","sections":[{"id":"","title":"Logs","level":1,"text":"Logs record all output from the client and the core — the most direct evidence when troubleshooting. User Guide record all output from the client and the core — the most direct evidence when troubleshooting. : keyword search and level filtering, with pause, clear and export; each line shows the rule and exit a connection matched."},{"id":"what-this-page-solves","title":"What this page solves","level":2,"text":"Failed subscription updates, config reload errors, a core that won't start — they all leave records here. When trouble hits, don't rush to change settings; come here first to see who reported the error and at which step."},{"id":"two-log-sources","title":"Two log sources","level":2,"text":"The view mixes two kinds of logs, both pushed in real time by the core and the client, arranged in time order: Source Contents Identified by Core logs Output from the mihomo core itself — connections, DNS, rule matching and so on. Pushed from the core over WebSocket. App logs The client's own actions — subscription updates, config reloads, core start/stop. Lines start with a [Unbound link] prefix. The prefix tells you who's speaking Lines with a [Unbound link] prefix are printed by the client; those without come from the core."},{"id":"filter-by-level","title":"Filter by level","level":2,"text":"Level filters sit at the top: Debug / Info / Warning / Error, matching the core log levels debug / info / warning / error. To focus, tick only the levels you care about — everything else is filtered out. Level Notes Debug The most detailed output; use it when troubleshooting. Info Routine operation records. Warning Notices that don't break anything but deserve attention. Error Lines that truly failed — handle these first."},{"id":"adjusting-the-log-level","title":"Adjusting the log level","level":2,"text":"Filtering only changes what's shown, not what's recorded. For more detailed logs, adjust Settings → Network → Log level; options are Debug / Info / Warning / Error / Silent. The right way to troubleshoot Switch the level to Debug first, then reproduce the problem, so the full sequence around the failure gets recorded; once found, set it back to Info to keep logs from bloating."},{"id":"suggested-troubleshooting-order","title":"Suggested troubleshooting order","level":2,"text":"With lots of logs, reading in this order is most efficient: First find error lines with the [Unbound link] prefix — they point straight at the client step that failed. Then the core's error lines — the concrete failures at the address, rule or connection level. Finally warning lines — to check for overlooked risks. For symptom-by-symptom fixes see Troubleshooting; if you suspect a network-layer problem, use Diagnostics alongside it."},{"id":"logs-persisted-to-files","title":"Logs persisted to files","level":2,"text":"Besides the on-screen view, logs are written in real time to logs\\app.log in the data directory for complete records. Files rotate by size: when full, they're archived as app.1.log, app.2.log and so on; archives older than the retention window are cleaned up automatically. Which levels are recorded, file size, file count and retention days are all set under Settings → Maintenance → App logs; \"Open log folder\" takes you straight there. Note this is separate from Settings → Network → Log level (which controls core logs)."}]},{"href":"diagnose.html","title":"Diagnostics","lead":"Diagnostics checks the core, ports, system proxy, DNS, exits and leaks in one pass, with a conclusion and suggestion for each item, plus the network rescue kit.","sections":[{"id":"","title":"Diagnostics","level":1,"text":"Diagnostics checks the core, ports, system proxy, DNS, exits and leaks in one pass, with a conclusion and suggestion for each item, plus the network rescue kit. User Guide checks the core, ports, system proxy, DNS, exits and leaks in one pass, with a conclusion and suggestion for each item, plus the network rescue kit. : checks the core, ports, system proxy, DNS, direct / proxy exits and WebRTC leaks one by one, with causes and advice for anything abnormal; the network rescue kit sits below."},{"id":"what-this-page-solves","title":"What this page solves","level":2,"text":"When pages won't load or the proxy seems ineffective, no need to guess. Entering the page starts the checks automatically; when done, the header summarizes results — e.g., \"11 passed\", \"N notices\", \"N issues\" — so you see the scale of the problem first. The \"Re-run checks\" button sits top-right and shows \"Checking…\" while running. Diagnostics only inspects; it never changes system settings — whether and what to repair is up to you."},{"id":"the-11-automatic-checks","title":"The 11 automatic checks","level":2,"text":"There are 11 checks; each gives a conclusion, and abnormal ones show \"Suggestion: …\" telling you the next step. Check What it inspects Core status Whether the core is running, its version, and whether it's elevated. Port listening Whether the mixed and control ports are taken by other programs. System proxy Whether the system proxy is on and points at the local mixed port. DNS resolution Resolution time and the DNS addresses in use. DNS takeover Whether TUN mode takes over system DNS resolution. Direct exit The exit IP probed when connecting directly. Proxy exit The exit IP probed through the proxy; if it matches the direct exit, it warns that the exit IP equals the direct one. WebRTC leaks Checks for public candidate IPs and warns when the real IP could leak. Current node The current node's latency; when unreachable it suggests picking a working node on the Proxies page. Direct GitHub access Direct reachability, timing and HTTP status code. Config loading The number of groups and nodes."},{"id":"network-rescue-kit","title":"Network rescue kit","level":2,"text":"The \"Network rescue kit\" section sits below, described as: \"When the proxy exits unexpectedly, DNS breaks or pages won't load: detect first to locate the problem, then repair as prompted.\" It offers two buttons: \"One-click rescue\" and \"Start check / Re-check\". One-click rescue does two things at once: reset the system proxy and flush the DNS cache. Neither needs admin rights and both take effect immediately — good for the vast majority of temporary network failures. Start check / Re-check runs the dedicated outage checks on their own. Detect first to locate the problem, then decide whether to use one-click rescue."},{"id":"outage-specific-checks","title":"Outage-specific checks","level":2,"text":"The dedicated checks cover five classes of problems, each with a matching repair button: Problem Symptom Repair button Leftover system proxy The proxy still points at 127.0.0.1:7890 while the core isn't running. Reset system proxy DNS resolution failure Domains can't be resolved. Flush DNS cache (ipconfig /flushdns, no admin needed) Abnormal DNS servers The adapter is set to invalid addresses like 0.0.0.0. Reset DNS servers / set to automatic (admin required) Abnormal hosts file Custom entries exist, or the file is missing. Restore hosts file (backs up the current content first, then resets to default; admin required) Direct network unreachable No internet even without the proxy. Reset network stack (resets winsock and TCP/IP; admin required; takes effect after a reboot)"},{"id":"privileges-and-confirmation","title":"Privileges and confirmation","level":2,"text":"Repairs needing admin rights trigger a UAC elevation prompt and only run once approved. Dangerous operations ask for confirmation first, with a red \"Run\" button — read the prompt before clicking. After a repair, the page re-runs the checks automatically to refresh the conclusions. Suggested troubleshooting order Start with the first four checks — core / ports / system proxy / DNS resolution. Once those pass, move on to the exit and leak checks — the scope narrows step by step."}]},{"href":"unlock.html","title":"Unlock Test","lead":"Unlock Test visits 18 popular services through the current exit and reports Unlocked / Partial / Blocked / Failed for each, so you can tell whether this route can stream and use AI tools.","sections":[{"id":"","title":"Unlock Test","level":1,"text":"Unlock Test visits 18 popular services through the current exit and reports Unlocked / Partial / Blocked / Failed for each, so you can tell whether this route can stream and use AI tools. User Guide visits 18 popular services through the current exit and reports Unlocked / Partial / Blocked / Failed for each, so you can tell whether this route can stream and use AI tools. : pick an exit node and 18 popular services are tested in one go, with \"Unlocked / Partial / Blocked\" badges as the verdict; each card can be retested on its own."},{"id":"what-this-page-solves","title":"What this page solves","level":2,"text":"\"I switched to a new node — can I still watch Netflix?\" \"ChatGPT suddenly won't sign in?\" Questions like these do not need a website-by-website check. This page tests all 18 services in one pass and reports the results without delay. Requests leave through the local mixed port — the same proxy path your daily traffic takes — so what you see is the real result of the current exit node; switch to another route and the outcome can be completely different."},{"id":"how-to-use-it","title":"How to use it","level":2,"text":"Pick the exit: the \"Exit node\" row at the top has two dropdowns — pick a group, then a node; you can switch right here, and each node shows its latency; Start a test: click \"Test all\" at the top right to check all 18 in parallel, or click the refresh icon on a single card to test just that service; Read the results: the subtitle summarizes \"Unlocked N · Partial N · Tested N/18\" in real time, and each card shows a status badge and a short note."},{"id":"what-the-states-mean","title":"What the states mean","level":2,"text":"Status Meaning Not tested Not tested yet. Click the refresh icon on the card to run a single check. Unlocked The service is fully available through the current exit. Partial Reachable, but with limited content — the classic case is Netflix showing only its own productions. Blocked Clearly unavailable; the detail line shows the HTTP status code or the region notice. Failed The request itself did not complete (timeout, proxy unreachable, etc.) — this does not mean the service is unavailable. Check the core and node status first, then test again."},{"id":"how-the-checks-work","title":"How the checks work","level":2,"text":"Every check sends requests through the local mixed port with a browser User-Agent and a 12-second timeout. Each service is judged differently: Netflix: two titles are cross-checked — an original series first, and only if it returns 200, a non-original title. Both reachable means Unlocked; only the original means Partial; otherwise Blocked. Disney+ / Prime Video / Spotify / TikTok: the home page loads and shows no \"not available in your region\" notice — Unlocked. Hulu / HBO Max: a redirect to a region notice page, or restricted-content text on the page, means Blocked. ChatGPT Web: a 403 response means the region is unsupported; ChatGPT iOS probes a long-stable mobile status endpoint instead. Bilibili: an in-site API reports how Bilibili sees the exit region — mainland China gets the full catalogue, while Hong Kong, Macao, Taiwan and overseas regions are partially restricted. Claude / Copilot / Gemini / X / iQIYI / Tencent Video / NetEase Cloud Music: home page reachability; region limits show up as a 403 or a redirect to a notice page."},{"id":"supported-services","title":"Supported services","level":2,"text":"18 services in total, covering overseas streaming, AI services and China-side platforms: Service Category What is checked Netflix Streaming Original + non-original title cross-check; detects \"own productions only\". Disney+ Streaming Home page reachability and region notice. YouTube Premium Streaming Region availability of the Premium subscription page. Prime Video Streaming Home page reachability. Spotify Streaming Home page reachability. TikTok Streaming Home page reachability. Hulu Streaming US only; detects region redirects. HBO Max Streaming Detects the region guidance page. ChatGPT Web AI services Web app reachability (403 detection included). ChatGPT iOS AI services Mobile status endpoint, a long-stable check target. Claude AI services Home page reachability; a 403 means the region is unsupported. Gemini AI services Redirects to a Google help page when the region is unsupported. Copilot AI services Home page reachability; a 403 means the region is unsupported. X (Twitter) Social Home page reachability. Bilibili China-side platforms In-site API region check (mainland / HK-Macao-Taiwan / overseas). iQIYI China-side platforms Home page reachability. Tencent Video China-side platforms Home page reachability. NetEase Cloud Music China-side platforms Home page reachability. In one sentence Unlock Test does not ask \"does this service exist\" but \"what happens right now when I reach it through this route\". Results reflect the exit as of the moment they were measured — retest after switching nodes."}]},{"href":"settings.html","title":"Settings","lead":"Ports, mode, TUN, DNS, shortcuts, app logs and backups all live here, split into six tabs: Appearance / System / Network / Tools / Maintenance / About. Each tab is grouped by function, with setting cards under each group title. This page explains every switch's effect and default value.","sections":[{"id":"","title":"Settings","level":1,"text":"Ports, mode, TUN, DNS, shortcuts, app logs and backups all live here, split into six tabs: Appearance / System / Network / Tools / Maintenance / About. Each tab is grouped by function, with setting cards under each group title. This page explains every switch's effect and default value. User Guide Ports, mode, TUN, DNS, shortcuts, app logs and backups all live here, split into six tabs: Appearance / System / Network / Tools / Maintenance / About. Each tab is grouped by function, with setting cards under each group title. This page explains every switch's effect and default value. is split into six tabs — Appearance / System / Network / Tools / Maintenance / About; the Appearance tab switches theme and language, and \"Theme customization\" adjusts accent and other colors, fonts and custom CSS. All settings save instantly Every switch and input saves immediately; the footer also reads \"All settings save instantly\" — no OK / Apply buttons. Core-related changes (ports, TUN, DNS) hot-reload or restart the core automatically."},{"id":"appearance","title":"Appearance","level":2,"text":"The Appearance tab has one group, \"Appearance and theme\": the appearance card manages theme, language and UI behavior; the theme customization card manages colors, fonts and custom CSS."},{"id":"appearance-2","title":"Appearance","level":3,"text":"Setting Options Notes Theme Dark / light / follow system The UI updates instantly on switching; no restart. Language Simplified Chinese The UI language — 13 built in, plus follow-system; applies immediately. Pause rendering when unfocused On Pauses traffic chart refresh when the window loses focus to cut CPU and memory use; recording continues and refresh resumes on focus. Proxies page columns Auto Cards per row on the Proxies page, 1–4; \"Auto\" fits the window width. Hide timed-out nodes Off When on, timed-out nodes are hidden on the Proxies page; the same setting as \"Hide timed-out\" at the top of that page."},{"id":"theme-customization","title":"Theme customization","level":3,"text":"Beyond the three themes, open \"Theme customization\" to fine-tune the UI: eight color categories, a custom UI font and custom CSS, all applied instantly. \"Restore defaults\" clears every customization (with confirmation). Setting Default Notes Brand / info / warning / error / success colors Default palette The main colors for buttons, selected states, notices and latency; leave blank for defaults. Secondary color Default palette Color for secondary emphasis and hover backgrounds; leave blank for the default. Primary / secondary text color Default palette Text colors for body and headings, and for captions and de-emphasized info; leave blank for defaults. UI font Default font stack Custom UI font family in CSS font-family syntax; blank uses the default stack. Custom CSS Empty An extra global stylesheet for fine-tuning details; clear it to recover from mistakes."},{"id":"system","title":"System","level":2,"text":"Two groups, \"Proxy and startup\" (system proxy, startup) and \"Tray and watchdog\" (tray and floating window, background watchdog), with global shortcuts at the end."},{"id":"system-proxy","title":"System proxy","level":3,"text":"Setting Default Notes System proxy Off Writes Windows Internet settings so programs that honor the system proxy use it automatically. Requires the core to be running. Proxy bypass list Empty Semicolon / comma-separated wildcard entries (e.g. *.local;192.168.*) whose addresses bypass the proxy; blank keeps the system's existing config. Shown only when the system proxy is on. Terminal proxy command — Pick PowerShell / Cmd / Bash, click \"Copy command\" and run it in a terminal so CLI tools (git / npm / curl, etc.) use the proxy temporarily."},{"id":"startup","title":"Startup","level":3,"text":"Setting Default Notes Launch at startup Off Runs Unbound link automatically after boot. Silent start Off On auto-start, skip the main window and go straight to the tray. Start page Dashboard The page shown after launch: Dashboard / Proxies / Connections / Traffic / Logs / Unlock Test / Settings. Startup script Not selected A script run automatically at app launch: exe / bat / cmd / ps1; skipped and logged if missing."},{"id":"tray-and-floating-window","title":"Tray and floating window","level":3,"text":"Setting Default Notes Tray click action Show main window What a left-click on the tray icon does (right-click always opens the quick panel): show main window / toggle system proxy / toggle TUN mode. Tray icons (three states) Built-in icons The tray icon changes with status: normal / system proxy on / TUN on. Each state can use your own PNG icon instead of the built-in one; clear it anytime to restore the default. Minimize to tray on close On The close button hides the window instead of quitting; the app keeps running in the background. Traffic floating window Off Shows a draggable live-speed window on the desktop. Floating window mouse pass-through Off When on, the floating window doesn't intercept the mouse; clicks pass through to windows below."},{"id":"background-watchdog","title":"Background watchdog","level":3,"text":"Setting Default Notes System notifications On Shows system notifications for events like core crashes and subscription auto-updates. Core watchdog On Restarts the core automatically after unexpected exits. Debounced — repeated crashes in a short time won't restart forever. Check for updates on launch On Checks for new client versions after launch and sends a notification when one is found. Clear connections on node switch On Closes old connections after switching nodes so the new node takes over existing sessions immediately. System proxy guard On Automatically restores system proxy settings if another program tampers with them, logging the event."},{"id":"global-shortcuts","title":"Global shortcuts","level":3,"text":"Click a shortcut to record a new combination; \"Restore defaults\" is provided. Shortcuts register at the system level — if another app already uses one, binding fails; just pick another. Action Default shortcut Toggle system proxy Ctrl+Shift+P Toggle TUN mode Ctrl+Shift+T Show main window Ctrl+Shift+U Toggle traffic floating window Ctrl+Shift+O Cycle proxy mode (Rule → Global → Direct) Ctrl+Shift+M Update all subscriptions Unbound; record your own Restart core Unbound; record your own"},{"id":"network","title":"Network","level":2,"text":"Three groups: \"Core and control\" (ports and mode, kernel behavior, web dashboard), \"Connection method\" (TUN mode, DNS), and \"Automation\" (speed test and health, network environment)."},{"id":"ports-and-mode","title":"Ports and mode","level":3,"text":"Setting Default Notes Mixed proxy port 7890 The HTTP / SOCKS mixed listening port; the core restarts automatically after a change. External Controller port 9090 The control API port; the core restarts automatically after a change. Mode Rule Rule / Global / Direct. \"Rule\" routes traffic by rules; \"Global\" sends all traffic through one exit; \"Direct\" bypasses the proxy entirely. Log level Info Debug / Info / Warning / Error / Silent; controls how detailed the core's log output is."},{"id":"kernel-behavior","title":"Kernel behavior","level":3,"text":"Setting Default Notes Auto-start core On Starts the core automatically when the app launches. Allow LAN connections Off When on, other devices on the same LAN can use the proxy via local-IP:mixed-port; the address is shown in the UI. Share to phone — Appears on the same row once Allow LAN is on. Clicking it shows a QR code (with the local proxy address); when the phone and PC are on the same LAN, set the phone's Wi-Fi \"Manual proxy\" to that address as prompted. IPv6 Off Lets the core use IPv6 for outbound traffic; keep it off when your network doesn't support IPv6 for better compatibility. Unified latency On Multiple nodes on the same host report one consistent latency value, making test results more stable and intuitive."},{"id":"web-dashboard","title":"Web dashboard","level":3,"text":"A built-in MetaCubeXD dashboard, downloaded automatically on first open (about 2.5MB). The local address is http://127.0.0.1:9090/ui/; click \"Open dashboard\" to enter. With \"Allow LAN connections\" on, a LAN dashboard address such as http://192.168.x.x:9090/ui/ also appears here, ready to copy to other devices on the same network with one click. The control secret protects the External Controller: randomly generated on first run and regenerable with one click; after a change the core restarts automatically, and dashboards or third-party clients using the old secret must switch to the new one."},{"id":"tun-mode","title":"TUN mode","level":3,"text":"A privilege badge sits next to the title: green \"Elevated\" with admin rights, otherwise orange \"Not elevated\", along with a \"Restart as administrator\" button. Setting Default Notes Enable TUN Off Takes over all traffic through a virtual adapter, covering programs that ignore the system proxy (some games, command-line tools). Requires admin rights. TUN stack Mixed Options are Mixed / Gvisor / System. Use Mixed when unsure."},{"id":"dns","title":"DNS","level":3,"text":"Setting Default Notes Custom DNS Off When on, overrides the DNS config from your subscription. DNS server list 223.5.5.5、119.29.29.29 One address per line. fake-ip acceleration On Recommended with TUN mode; when off, redir-host mode is used. Fallback DNS Empty Servers to fall back on when the main DNS is unreliable, in the same format as the main DNS; left blank, nothing is sent. Static Hosts Empty One \"domain IP\" mapping per line; supports # comments and is delivered to the core with the config. DNS-related changes reload the core."},{"id":"speed-test-and-health","title":"Speed test and health","level":3,"text":"Setting Default Notes Scheduled latency test Off Automatically tests the current group at the set interval to keep latency data fresh. Test interval 5 minutes Interval for automatic latency tests, from 1 to 1440 minutes. Node health monitoring On Keeps a rolling latency history per node and draws trends on the Proxies page; sends a system notification when the current node keeps failing tests. Automatic failover Off When the current node fails 2 latency tests in a row, it switches automatically to the fallback node; relies on Node health monitoring above. Applies only to \"manual select\" proxy groups — auto-test groups recover on their own inside the core. Fallback node Auto-select Preferred target for failover; the dropdown lists the nodes in the main proxy group. When left empty, the available node with the lowest latency in the group is chosen automatically. Shown once Automatic failover is enabled."},{"id":"network-environment","title":"Network environment","level":3,"text":"Switches the system proxy and mode automatically according to the connected Wi-Fi name — handy for \"on at home, off at the office\". The app checks the Wi-Fi name every 10 seconds; when it changes, the first enabled rule that matches is applied from top to bottom. Actions identical to the current state are skipped, so manual adjustments are not overridden. This feature does not change the TUN state. Setting Default Notes Network environment automation Off Master switch; when on, the matching rule below is applied whenever the Wi-Fi name changes. Current Wi-Fi — Shows the name of the connected network (\"Not connected to Wi-Fi\" when there is none); the button to the right refreshes it manually. Rule list Empty One rule per row: the switch on the left enables it, followed by the Wi-Fi name, the system-proxy action and the mode action. Names support * (any characters) and ? (a single character) wildcards, case-insensitive, e.g. Home-*. An action set to \"No change\" keeps the current state; other choices (system proxy: On / Off; mode: Rule / Global / Direct) are applied on a match. Add rule — The button at the top right adds a rule that is enabled by default with all actions set to \"No change\"; fill in the name and actions to take effect."},{"id":"tools","title":"Tools","level":2,"text":"Four utilities around rules and connections: lifting the loopback restriction for Store apps, updating the rules database, setting up port-forwarding tunnels, and tidying the node list with keyword filters and replacement rules."},{"id":"uwp-app-tool","title":"UWP app tool","level":3,"text":"Apps installed from the Microsoft Store can't reach the local proxy address (127.0.0.1) by default, which shows up as no network inside the app. Click \"Manage apps\" to open the list and flip the switch for the apps you need — on lifts the loopback restriction, off restores it. Changing loopback exemptions requires admin rights; if it fails, relaunch the app as administrator and retry."},{"id":"geodata-databases","title":"GeoData databases","level":3,"text":"GEOIP / GEOSITE routing depends on four database files (geoip.dat, geosite.dat, geoip.metadb, GeoLite2-ASN.mmdb); the core loads them from the data directory at startup, and missing or outdated files break the corresponding rules. This section shows each file's size and update time; click \"Update databases\" to fetch them from the official release page in one go — on direct-connection failure an accelerated mirror is tried automatically, and if that still fails the download goes through the local proxy. Updating all files at once is a large download, so give it a moment."},{"id":"traffic-tunnels","title":"Traffic tunnels","level":3,"text":"Forwards a local port to a remote target: the core listens on the given local port and relays through a chosen exit (proxy group or node) to the target host — handy for mapping remote intranet services (databases, remote desktop) to your machine. Each tunnel picks a protocol (TCP / UDP / mixed), a local listen address and port, and a target address and port; with the exit left blank, traffic routes by rules. Tunnels are runtime config; the core restarts automatically after any change."},{"id":"node-cleanup","title":"Node Cleanup","level":3,"text":"Applies a unified cleanup to the node list when the runtime config is generated — handy when a subscription brings too many noisy nodes. The three parts combine freely: Filter keywords removes any node whose name hits a keyword (case-insensitive); Rename rules replace text in node names in order (case-sensitive; an empty replacement deletes that piece of text); Common prefix / suffix adds a uniform prefix and suffix to every node name after all replacements. The cleanup also rewrites node references in proxy groups, rules and tunnels, so renamed nodes never break their references. Click \"Preview\" to see the result before deciding whether to enable it: how many nodes in total, how many filtered, how many renamed — preview works even while the switch is off."},{"id":"maintenance","title":"Maintenance","level":2,"text":"Two groups: \"Updates and tests\" (subscription, core update, node latency test) and \"Data and logs\" (config snapshots, backup and restore, app logs)."},{"id":"subscription","title":"Subscription","level":3,"text":"Auto-update interval, in hours, 0 means off, off by default. Once enabled, every subscription in the list updates on schedule in the background; when done, the config reloads automatically and a system notification is sent. Auto-optimize after update, off by default. Once enabled, every time subscriptions finish updating and the config reloads, the client automatically tests the main proxy group and sets the lowest-latency available node as the current node (applies only to \"manual select\" proxy groups — auto-test groups make their own pick inside the core); the result is sent as a system notification. Profile usage alerts is on by default and relies on the traffic and expiry info provided by the subscription source. There are three tiers: Usage alert threshold (alert when used traffic reaches this percentage, default 80%, adjustable from 1 to 100), Expiration alert (alert when remaining days are at or below this value, default 3 days, 0 turns this tier off) and the \"expired\" alert. Each tier fires only once; after a renewal or plan reset it arms again and alerts again when the condition is met."},{"id":"core-update","title":"Core update","level":3,"text":"Shows the current core version; click \"Check for updates\" to see if a new one exists. When there is, update with one click — the client downloads from GitHub Releases and replaces the core."},{"id":"node-latency-test","title":"Node latency test","level":3,"text":"Setting Default Notes Test URL http://www.gstatic.com/generate_204 A lightweight 204 URL is recommended. Test timeout 5000 ms A node that doesn't respond within this time counts as timed out."},{"id":"config-snapshots","title":"Config snapshots","level":3,"text":"Before each runtime config is generated, the previous config.yaml is archived automatically (the latest 10 are kept). Click View snapshots to see the list; if the core won't start or the config misbehaves, roll back to the last working version in one click. Restoring writes back and hot-reloads the core immediately, archiving the current file first. Snapshots are an emergency fallback: after restoring, the config is regenerated from your current settings the next time you change something or restart the core."},{"id":"backup-and-restore","title":"Backup and restore","level":3,"text":"Action Notes Export backup Exports settings, subscriptions, Merge config and the core version into a single JSON file. Import backup Restores all data from a backup file, overwriting current settings and subscriptions. A confirmation \"Import backup?\" appears first. Export diagnostics Packs app and core versions, current settings, runtime config and recent logs into a ZIP file, with sensitive fields such as secrets masked automatically — handy to attach to a bug report."},{"id":"app-logs","title":"App logs","level":3,"text":"The client's own logs are written to logs\\app.log in the data directory and rotate by size into archives (app.1.log, app.2.log…); click \"Open log folder\" to view them. They record the app's own actions (core start/stop, system proxy changes, config and updates), complementing the core's real-time logs on the Logs page — ideal for after-the-fact troubleshooting. Setting Default Notes Log level Warning Error / Warning / Info / Debug; controls how detailed the log files are, effective immediately. Max file size 128 KB Range: 16 KB - 10 MB; files rotate automatically at the limit. File count 8 Range: 2 - 50; the total number of log files kept, with the oldest deleted beyond that. Retention days 7 Range: 1 - 365; archives older than this are cleaned up automatically."},{"id":"about","title":"About","level":2,"text":"The app logo and name sit at the top, with app version, core version and data directory listed below. The data directory is %APPDATA%\\com.unboundlink.desktop, copyable with one click — back it up before migrating machines or reinstalling; click \"Open folder\" to view it in Explorer. There is an Exit app button — confirming 'Exit Unbound link?' truly ends the program. Check for updates lets you download the installer in the app and complete the upgrade automatically (it installs silently and restarts with the new version); you can cancel and download manually at any time. Help & support opens the website and the online guide. The Developer tools entry opens a built-in debugging panel — not needed for daily use. The bottom of the page reminds you that all settings are saved instantly. Summary The three most-used spots: System → the system proxy card; Network → Mode under \"Ports and mode\"; Maintenance → Data and logs → Export backup. All of them save instantly with no extra confirmation."}]},{"href":"advanced.html","title":"Advanced tips","lead":"This page collects six handy techniques: fine-tune subscriptions with Merge and scripts, create quick rules for domains, route restricted nodes through a relay, jump around with the command palette, and get the floating window and shortcuts set up your way.","sections":[{"id":"","title":"Advanced tips","level":1,"text":"This page collects six handy techniques: fine-tune subscriptions with Merge and scripts, create quick rules for domains, route restricted nodes through a relay, jump around with the command palette, and get the floating window and shortcuts set up your way. User Guide This page collects six handy techniques: fine-tune subscriptions with Merge and scripts, create quick rules for domains, route restricted nodes through a relay, jump around with the command palette, and get the floating window and shortcuts set up your way."},{"id":"how-to-use-merge-override","title":"How to use Merge override","level":2,"text":"On the Profiles page, open \"Global override config\" and write a YAML snippet in the editor. When the runtime config is generated, the snippet is deeply merged recursively with the subscription to override its fields — write only what you want to change; no need to copy the whole config, and subscription updates remain unaffected. Don't put port, control secret, mode or log level here These are app-level settings written by the client when generating the config, overriding same-named entries in Merge. Change them under Settings → Network → Ports and mode. The snippet below does three things at once: enables unified delay, appends a dns block, and adds a node to the proxy group named \"Manual switch\". unified-delay: true dns: enable: true nameserver: - 223.5.5.5 proxy-groups: - name: Manual switch type: select proxies: - HK Node 01"},{"id":"extension-script-examples","title":"Extension script examples","level":2,"text":"\"Global extension script\" on the same page accepts QuickJS scripts in the editor; the script must implement main(config) and return config. It runs on the built-in QuickJS engine to batch-rename nodes and add or remove rules. The two snippets below can be pasted as-is. Example 1: strip ads from node names. function main(config){ config.proxies.forEach(p => { p.name = p.name.replace(/official|subscribe/gi, \"\") }) return config } Example 2: add a uniform prefix to all node names. function main(config){ config.proxies.forEach(p => { p.name = \"[U] \" + p.name }) return config }"},{"id":"quick-rules","title":"Quick rules","level":2,"text":"On the Connections page, right-click a connection and choose \"direct for this domain\" or \"route this domain through a chosen line\". The client generates a DOMAIN-SUFFIX rule, inserted at the top of the rule list — higher priority than subscription rules, effective immediately."},{"id":"chained-proxy","title":"Chained proxy","level":2,"text":"On the Proxies page click \"Chained proxy rules\" in the toolbar to open the chain editor: saved chains on the left; on the right, build a chain by picking hops from all candidates, with drag-to-reorder, insert-between-hops, region filters and pinned favorites — each candidate shows recent latency, and a proxy group can only be the first hop. Chains can be any length and apply immediately via hot reload after saving. To quickly give a single node a front proxy, just click the chain button on its card. Chained latency is roughly the sum of all hops — usually slower, but potentially more stable."},{"id":"floating-window-and-shortcuts","title":"Floating window and shortcuts","level":2,"text":"The traffic floating window is customizable in content and appearance: Item Options Size compact / standard / large. Layout card / capsule / gauge. Color theme auto (follows the main app) / graphite / aurora / sunset / emerald / midnight / ivory — 7 in total. Display items Current node latency, active connections, mini traffic chart, today's traffic, system proxy status and mode — each toggleable. Opacity Default 88. Default global shortcuts are listed in Settings: toggle the system proxy Ctrl+Shift+P, toggle TUN mode Ctrl+Shift+T, show the main window Ctrl+Shift+U, toggle the traffic floating window Ctrl+Shift+O and cycle the proxy mode Ctrl+Shift+M; \"Update all subscriptions\" and \"Restart core\" are unbound by default and can be recorded in Settings. Right-clicking the tray icon also opens a quick panel for toggling the system proxy or TUN, opening the main window and more; clicking the \"node\" or \"group\" row pops up a standalone node picker beside the panel — all proxy groups on the left, that group's nodes and latencies on the right, click to switch without digging through the small panel."},{"id":"global-command-palette","title":"Global command palette","level":2,"text":"Press Ctrl+K (or click the search button in the sidebar) to bring up the command palette on any page; type keywords to filter the entries, or leave it empty to list every available action by group: Group What it does Pages Jump to any page in the app. Settings Jump straight to any of the six settings tabs: Appearance / System / Network / Tools / Maintenance / About. Actions Start / stop / restart the core, toggle the system proxy, TUN mode, LAN connections and the floating window, switch light/dark themes and rule / global / direct modes, quit the app, and more. Subscription Update all subscriptions; update, enable or disable a specific subscription; open the provider's website in one click when one is set. Maintenance Check for core updates (upgrade in one click when one is found), check for app updates, update the GeoData database, export and import backups, back up to the cloud or restore from it (shown once WebDAV is configured), and reset rule-hit, app or node traffic statistics. Network rescue One-click rescue, flush the DNS cache, reset the system proxy and DNS servers, restore the hosts file, reset the network stack; risky actions ask for confirmation first. Tools Open the web dashboard, the data directory and the log directory, export diagnostics, and open the official site or the User Guide. Nodes Search existing nodes — results show recent latency, press Enter to switch (appears only after you type a keyword, keeping the list short for large subscriptions). Keyboard: ↑ ↓ to select, Enter to run, Esc to close. Everyday moves like switching nodes or modes, checking for updates or restoring backups no longer need the mouse."}]},{"href":"troubleshooting.html","title":"Troubleshooting","lead":"Common problems below follow Symptom → Cause → Fix, each with concrete steps — just work through them in order.","sections":[{"id":"","title":"Troubleshooting","level":1,"text":"Common problems below follow Symptom → Cause → Fix, each with concrete steps — just work through them in order. User Guide Common problems below follow Symptom → Cause → Fix, each with concrete steps — just work through them in order. Start troubleshooting from \"Stability & → Diagnostics\": abnormal items come with causes and advice, and many failures can be fixed with one click in the network rescue kit."},{"id":"pages-wont-load-no-network","title":"Pages won't load / no network","level":2,"text":"Symptom: the browser can't open any page. Cause: leftover system proxy settings, a bad DNS cache or an unhealthy network stack. Fix: open Diagnostics and check the first few results; then run \"One-click rescue\" in the network rescue kit (reset system proxy + flush DNS cache, no admin rights needed). If that still doesn't help, follow the on-screen prompt to reset the network stack — that requires admin rights and takes effect after a reboot."},{"id":"the-proxy-isnt-taking-effect","title":"The proxy isn't taking effect","level":2,"text":"Symptom: the internet works, but traffic doesn't go through the proxy. Cause: the mode was switched to Global or Direct; the system proxy is off; the core isn't running. Fix: open Settings → Network and check the mode is \"Rule\" (choosing \"Global\" or \"Direct\" changes behavior); open Settings → System → System proxy and check it's on; then confirm the core is running via the core status on the Dashboard or in Diagnostics."},{"id":"some-apps-bypass-the-proxy","title":"Some apps bypass the proxy","level":2,"text":"Symptom: the browser works, but some programs don't use the proxy — and apps from the Microsoft Store can't open pages at all. Cause: this is the inherent limitation of system proxies — some programs don't read the setting; Store apps are also subject to the Windows loopback restriction, which blocks access to the local address (127.0.0.1) by default. Fix: for Store apps, open Settings → Tools → UWP app tool and flip the switch to lift the loopback restriction (needs admin rights); for other programs switch to TUN mode (see Settings → Network → TUN mode), or bind the process to a chosen line with App Routing."},{"id":"all-nodes-time-out","title":"All nodes time out","level":2,"text":"Symptom: every node tests as timed out. Cause: the nodes are temporarily unavailable, or the subscription has expired or is stale. Fix: first click \"Test all\" on the Proxies page; check whether the subscription has expired and click the \"Traffic\" button on its card under Profiles to refresh the info; then check the \"direct exit / proxy exit\" and \"current node\" conclusions in Diagnostics; you can also click \"Update\" for the subscription on the Profiles page. To clear useless timed-out nodes, turn on the \"Hide timed-out\" switch at the top of the Proxies page."},{"id":"port-already-in-use","title":"Port already in use","level":2,"text":"Symptom: the core can't listen; a port conflict is reported. Cause: the default port is taken by another program. Fix: the \"port listening\" check in Diagnostics shows which port is taken; change the mixed proxy port or the control port under Settings → Network → Ports and mode — the core restarts automatically after changes."},{"id":"subscription-update-fails","title":"Subscription update fails","level":2,"text":"Symptom: clicking \"Update\" changes nothing. Cause: the subscription URL is unreachable, or the current network is down. Fix: confirm the subscription URL is reachable; check the error lines under Logs; if the network is down, use Diagnostics to locate the problem first."},{"id":"tun-wont-enable-not-elevated-warning","title":"TUN won't enable / \"Not elevated\" warning","level":2,"text":"Symptom: turning on \"Enable TUN\" has no effect, and the privilege badge shows an orange \"Not elevated\". Cause: the current process lacks admin rights, or the install directory is in use. Fix: go to Settings → Network → TUN mode and click \"Restart as administrator\", confirm the UAC prompt and retry; also make sure the install directory isn't in use."},{"id":"settings-changed-but-nothing-happened","title":"Settings changed but nothing happened","level":2,"text":"Symptom: changing a setting doesn't change the behavior. Cause: core-related changes need a reload or restart of the core; or config generation failed. Fix: core-related changes (ports, TUN, DNS) reload or restart the core automatically; if nothing changes, check Logs for config errors and, if needed, check the Merge override or extension script under Profiles — a syntax error makes config generation fail."},{"id":"data-and-backup","title":"Data and backup","level":2,"text":"Symptom: you need to migrate to a new machine or reinstall Windows. Cause: the data directory lives under the user folder on the system drive and is lost on reinstall. Fix: the data directory is %APPDATA%\\com.unboundlink.desktop. Before migrating or reinstalling, open Settings → Maintenance → Backup and restore → Export backup, then restore it in the new environment with \"Import backup\". When you need help troubleshooting, click \"Export diagnostics\" first and attach the ZIP to your report."},{"id":"the-core-wont-start","title":"The core won't start","level":2,"text":"Symptom: the core status never shows Running. Cause: the install directory is incomplete — the core sidecar is missing or damaged. Fix: make sure the install directory is complete (the core ships as a sidecar program with the installer); check Logs for errors; go to Settings → Maintenance → Core update and click \"Check for updates\" to re-fetch the core. Troubleshooting checklist Check the core status on the Dashboard. Check Logs for errors. Run Diagnostics and check the first 4 items. Use the network rescue kit. Check the mode and the system proxy. Check whether the subscription needs updating. Check for port conflicts. If the problem is hard to pin down, click Settings → Maintenance → Backup and restore → Export diagnostics and attach the ZIP to your report."}]}]};
